WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
A scholarship offer can look exciting.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.160
A missed delivery can feel urgent.

3
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.800
And a message from university support can sound convincing.

4
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.480
But what should you do before clicking, paying, or sharing a
code?

5
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.680
This is SecondLook.

6
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.440
We built it for the AI and cybersecurity track at ForgeHacks.

7
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.460
It gives you a private moment to inspect a message and choose how
to verify it.

8
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.640
Let's try the scholarship example.

9
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.780
The message asks for a processing fee sets a two-hour deadline
and says to keep it confidential.

10
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.140
We check it, then read the actual cues.

11
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:41.020
Each observation points to words in the message and explains why
that request deserves a pause.

12
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.980
The trained model adds another kind of evidence.

13
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.440
Language patterns it learned from public SMS examples.

14
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.560
You can expand its explanation to see which phrases increased the
spam pattern score.

15
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.080
That score is not the probability that a message is fraudulent.

16
00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.700
Next, a delivery message.

17
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.940
SecondLook notices the payment request, pressure, and unusual
link structure.

18
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.360
The address is displayed as text.

19
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.520
The app never opens it or sends your message to an external
service.

20
00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.840
An account message asks for a password and a verification code.

21
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.340
The next step is to contact the university through a channel you
already know,

22
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.520
rather than replying with private information.

23
00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.540
Now, compare an ordinary study group message.

24
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.260
It has fewer cues, but the interface still avoids saying safe.

25
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.000
The model cannot establish who sent it.

26
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.680
A short message does not give the model enough useful context.

27
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.000
Instead of showing a confident score, SecondLook explains the
limitation.

28
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:47.100
The verification guide helps you pause, find your own trusted
contact, and confirm the exact request.

29
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:53.060
If you have already acted, it points you toward the relevant
official support or payment provider.

30
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.100
Behind the interface is a real trained model.

31
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.320
TF-IDF word and phrase features followed by logistic regression.

32
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.940
We removed normalized duplicates before splitting the data set.

33
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:13.060
On 1,026 held out historical SMS messages, spam precision was
97.17%

34
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:16.540
and recall was 81.75%.

35
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.140
JavaScript inference matches Python across every held out
example.

36
00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.220
Those results are not modern scam accuracy.

37
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.980
SecondLook does not verify identities or detect AI-generated
content.

38
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.960
Its value is clear evidence and a practical next step with your
message staying on your device.

39
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.180
SecondLook, check before you click.
